From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 15:15:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] cpu/x86/smm: A page after the SMMSTORE com buffer for the
 arguments

SMM reads the arguments of an SMMSTORE command at the physical address
that the payload passes in EBX, and the data at the com buffer, both in
memory that the payload owns. EDK2 kept the arguments in runtime
services data, and macOS's boot loader moves runtime services code and
data after ExitBootServices(): SMM then read arguments, and from
ouroBIOS rc15 data too, where they were no longer. OpenCore's
AvoidRuntimeDefrag hid them from it. MMIO with EFI_MEMORY_RUNTIME stays
where it is.

The CBMEM entry of the com buffer is one page longer, for the arguments
that the payload passes at runtime: the payload reports the whole entry,
which the coreboot table gives it, as runtime MMIO. SMM gets the data
buffer as before, the size of a block.

TEST=QEMU q35 with SMMSTORE, DEBUG_SMI and the payload of the W541
(with edk2/0029): the CBMEM entry SMM COMBUFFER is 0x11000 bytes, which
the payload reports as MMIO with EFI_MEMORY_RUNTIME (memmap of a UEFI
shell, Linux 6.8 with efi=debug). Linux writes a variable through
efivarfs at runtime: SMM logs the raw writes with param = the com
buffer + 0x10000, and the next boot reads the variable. The W541 nri
ROM builds.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018n3yuWwDLW63F4vdf7ZFTv
---
 src/cpu/x86/smm/smm_module_loader.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/cpu/x86/smm/smm_module_loader.c b/src/cpu/x86/smm/smm_module_loader.c
index cd5017ca6a..6ceca2816c 100644
--- a/src/cpu/x86/smm/smm_module_loader.c
+++ b/src/cpu/x86/smm/smm_module_loader.c
@@ -359,7 +359,14 @@ static void setup_smihandler_params(struct smm_runtime *mod_params,
 			return;
 		}
 
-		void *ptr = cbmem_add(CBMEM_ID_SMM_COMBUFFER, info.block_size);
+		/*
+		 * One page more than the data buffer that SMM gets: the payload puts its
+		 * arguments there at runtime, in the same region, which it reports as
+		 * runtime MMIO. The boot loader of macOS moves the runtime services data,
+		 * not MMIO, and SMM reads the arguments at their physical address. The
+		 * coreboot table gives the payload the whole entry.
+		 */
+		void *ptr = cbmem_add(CBMEM_ID_SMM_COMBUFFER, info.block_size + 4 * KiB);
 		if (!ptr) {
 			printk(BIOS_ERR, "SMMSTORE: Failed to add com buffer\n");
 			return;
